trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The abdominal X-ray confirms distal intestinal obstruction (dilated loops + absent rectal gas). In a 2-day-old with bilious vomiting and failure to pass meconium, this strongly points to a surgical neonatal obstruction. Key differential diagnoses include:  
- Meconium ileus (especially if associated with cystic fibrosis — often presents with inspissated meconium causing ileal obstruction, may show “ground-glass” or “soap-bubble” appearance on X-ray, though not always visible);  
- Hirschsprung disease (functional obstruction due to aganglionosis; typically shows transition zone on contrast enema, but rectal gas is usually absent early on);  
- Ileal atresia or volvulus;  
- Meconium plug syndrome (less severe, often resolves with enema).  

To distinguish between these — particularly to identify a transition zone (Hirschsprung), confirm patency of the distal colon, or assess for microcolon — a contrast enema is the next most informative, readily available test. It is diagnostic for Hirschsprung (narrowed aganglionic segment with abrupt transition to dilated proximal bowel) and can also relieve meconium plug syndrome. It is also useful in evaluating for atresia or stenosis.

[TEST] Contrast enema

